1
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:18,020
While working in the laboratory, it will often be required to separate liquid mixtures or to purify liquid from non-volatile substances.

2
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:25,020
This separation or purification can be done by utilizing the liquid's boiling point through a technique called distillation.

3
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:31,590
Distillation works through the processes of vaporization and condensation.

4
00:00:31,590 --> 00:00:39,590
Vaporization is the transformation of a liquid to a gas, and condensation is the conversion of a gaseous species into a liquid.

5
00:00:40,590 --> 00:00:48,590
To perform a distillation, a mixture of two miscible liquids is heated until the liquid with the higher vapor pressure reaches its boiling point.

6
00:00:48,590 --> 00:00:54,590
This vapor travels up the inside of the flask, through a distilling head, and into a condenser.

7
00:00:54,590 --> 00:01:01,590
The cold water jacket on the condenser transforms the vapor back into liquid, which then travels into the receiving flask.

8
00:01:01,590 --> 00:01:08,590
In this manner, distillation effectively separates lower boiling point compounds from higher boiling point compounds.

9
00:01:09,590 --> 00:01:13,590
Three types of distillations are commonly used in the laboratory.

10
00:01:13,590 --> 00:01:19,590
Depending on your liquid solution, you will have to decide which type of distillation will be the most effective.

11
00:01:19,590 --> 00:01:23,590
The first type of distillation is a simple distillation.

12
00:01:23,590 --> 00:01:32,590
This technique is used when separating miscible liquids that boil below 150 degrees Celsius at one atmosphere from either non-volatile impurities

13
00:01:33,590 --> 00:01:38,590
or from a second liquid that boils at least 25 degrees Celsius higher than the first.

14
00:01:38,590 --> 00:01:42,590
The second type of distillation is the fractional distillation.

15
00:01:42,590 --> 00:01:49,590
This technique should be used to separate liquid mixtures that have less than a 25 degrees Celsius difference in boiling point.

16
00:01:49,590 --> 00:01:53,590
The third type of distillation is a vacuum distillation.

17
00:01:53,590 --> 00:02:01,590
This technique should be used to separate miscible liquids that boil above 150 degrees Celsius at one atmosphere

18
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:08,590
In this video, Distillation Part 1, we will discuss simple and fractional distillations.

19
00:02:08,590 --> 00:02:13,590
In Distillation Part 2, we will discuss vacuum distillations.

20
00:02:13,590 --> 00:02:21,590
To carry out a simple distillation, you will need a cork ring, a round bottom distilling flask, two ring stands,

21
00:02:21,590 --> 00:02:28,590
a stir bar and magnetic stirrer, a distilling head, a condenser, a vacuum adapter, grease,

22
00:02:28,590 --> 00:02:35,590
three yellow CAC clamps, a thermometer and thermometer adapter, three round bottom receiving flasks,

23
00:02:35,590 --> 00:02:43,590
two extension clamps and fasteners, a heating mantle, an iron ring, a variable voltage transformer or variac,

24
00:02:43,590 --> 00:02:49,590
three hose clips, two pieces of rubber tubing and a container for an ice water bath.

25
00:02:49,590 --> 00:02:55,590
The first step in performing a distillation is choosing the proper size distillation flask.

26
00:02:55,590 --> 00:03:01,590
The distilling flask should not be more than half full or less than one third full when the distillation begins.

27
00:03:01,590 --> 00:03:07,590
If the flask is overfilled, the solution will likely overheat or bump into the condenser.

28
00:03:07,590 --> 00:03:13,590
Bumping will cause impurities to be collected in the receiving flask, rendering the distillation worthless.

29
00:03:13,590 --> 00:03:19,590
If the flask is less than a third full, then you'll have a greater chance of losing some product

30
00:03:19,590 --> 00:03:24,590
because a substantial amount of the material will be needed just to fill the flask and distilling head with vapor.

31
00:03:24,590 --> 00:03:29,590
While we're on the topic of size, we'd like to speak briefly about heating mantles.

32
00:03:29,590 --> 00:03:34,590
If you do not have an appropriately sized heating mantle, then use one that is too big.

33
00:03:34,590 --> 00:03:41,590
Never use a heating mantle that is too small because heat is not easily transferred between the mantle and flask due to poor contact,

34
00:03:41,590 --> 00:03:44,590
which causes the mantle to burn out.

35
00:03:44,590 --> 00:03:49,590
If the mantle is too big for the flask, then add sand to fill in the empty space.

36
00:03:49,590 --> 00:03:56,590
After you have chosen a distilling flask, transfer your sample into it and add an appropriately sized stir bar.

37
00:03:56,590 --> 00:04:03,590
If you will not have access to a magnetic stirrer, then add a few boiling stones to the distilling flask instead.

38
00:04:03,590 --> 00:04:11,590
The next step is the assembly of the distillation apparatus, which is likely one of the most difficult glassware assemblies you'll ever encounter.

39
00:04:11,590 --> 00:04:17,590
The neck of the distilling flask is clamped onto one of the ring stands with an extension clamp and fastener.

40
00:04:17,590 --> 00:04:23,590
A heating mantle, which is plugged into the variac, rests on an iron ring below the distilling flask.

41
00:04:23,590 --> 00:04:28,590
If you're using a stir bar, then place a magnetic stirrer underneath the heating mantle.

42
00:04:28,590 --> 00:04:34,590
Leave enough room below the iron ring so that the heating mantle can be dropped away from the distilling flask if necessary.

43
00:04:34,590 --> 00:04:42,590
The distilling head is attached to the top of the distilling flask and a condenser is attached to the side arm of the distilling head.

44
00:04:42,590 --> 00:04:50,590
The vacuum adapter is attached to the other end of the condenser and this joint is attached to the second ring stand with an extension clamp and fastener.

45
00:04:50,590 --> 00:04:57,590
A small receiving flask is clamped to the vacuum adapter with a yellow kec clamp and an ice water bath is placed underneath.

46
00:04:57,590 --> 00:05:05,590
The thermometer and thermometer adapter are attached to the top of the distilling head and clamped in place with a green kec clamp.

47
00:05:05,590 --> 00:05:10,590
The top of the thermometer bulb should be aligned with the bottom of the side arm on the distillation head.

48
00:05:10,590 --> 00:05:14,590
Yellow kec clamps are used to secure both ends of the condenser.

49
00:05:14,590 --> 00:05:20,590
Rubber tubing is attached to the water inlet and outlet of the condenser and secured with hose clips.

50
00:05:20,590 --> 00:05:26,590
Finally, place two clean dry receiving flasks near the distillation apparatus.

51
00:05:26,590 --> 00:05:33,590
Before continuing, confirm that all of the joints have been lightly greased and that no kec clamps are used on any joint that will become hot.

52
00:05:34,590 --> 00:05:40,590
After assembling the distillation apparatus, attach the tubing at the bottom of the condenser to the water source.

53
00:05:40,590 --> 00:05:44,590
Place the end of the tubing at the top of the condenser into a sink.

54
00:05:44,590 --> 00:05:49,590
Remember, water goes in at the bottom and out at the top to discourage the formation of air bubbles.

55
00:05:49,590 --> 00:05:54,590
Double check that all of the joints are secured with hose clips and start the water flow.

56
00:05:54,590 --> 00:05:59,590
The water should flow at the slowest rate that is necessary to keep the condenser cold.

57
00:05:59,590 --> 00:06:06,590
If the flow rate is too fast, the chance of the tubing popping off the condenser and spraying all over your workspace greatly increases.

58
00:06:06,590 --> 00:06:12,590
To perform this simple distillation, turn on the variac and heat the sample to a gentle boil.

59
00:06:12,590 --> 00:06:15,590
Make sure that the vacuum adapter remains open to the air.

60
00:06:15,590 --> 00:06:18,590
Never heat a closed system or it will explode.

61
00:06:18,590 --> 00:06:23,590
A ring of condensate will move up the flask and into the distilling head.

62
00:06:23,590 --> 00:06:28,590
When the vapor reaches the height of the thermometer bulb, the temperature reading will jump dramatically,

63
00:06:28,590 --> 00:06:32,590
indicating the formation of a droplet of liquid on the thermometer bulb.

64
00:06:32,590 --> 00:06:39,590
If the condensate ring completely stops rising at any point, slightly increase the setting on the variac.

65
00:06:39,590 --> 00:06:47,590
After the vapor reaches the condenser, it transforms back into liquid, flows down the condenser, and into a small receiving flask.

66
00:06:47,590 --> 00:06:53,590
When approximately 10 drops have been collected in the small receiving flask, quickly replace it with the larger one.

67
00:06:53,590 --> 00:07:00,590
The first couple of drops of a distillation are always discarded because they may contain some lower boiling impurities.

68
00:07:00,590 --> 00:07:06,590
At this point, the distillate should be dropping into the receiving flask at a rate of 10 drops per minute.

69
00:07:06,590 --> 00:07:13,590
If the rate is faster, then the heat is too high and higher boiling point impurities may have escaped into the receiving flask.

70
00:07:13,590 --> 00:07:19,590
If the rate is too slow, then the distillation will occur over too long of a time period.

71
00:07:19,590 --> 00:07:27,590
After the lower boiling liquid has been completely distilled, no drop of liquid will be on the thermometer bulb and the temperature will plunge dramatically.

72
00:07:27,590 --> 00:07:35,590
At this point, immediately replace the large receiving flask with a new small receiving flask to collect the last few drops of liquid.

73
00:07:35,590 --> 00:07:41,590
These drops may contain some higher boiling impurities that should not be collected in the main receiving flask.

74
00:07:41,590 --> 00:07:48,590
Record the temperature range of when the mixture first started to boil until right before the dramatic drop in temperature.

75
00:07:48,590 --> 00:07:51,590
This is the boiling point of the distillate.

76
00:07:51,590 --> 00:07:56,590
If the range is within 2 degrees Celsius, your distillate is fairly pure.

77
00:07:56,590 --> 00:08:00,590
After you have collected the final few drops of liquid, turn off the heat source.

78
00:08:00,590 --> 00:08:05,590
A simple distillation is performed more effectively if certain guidelines are followed.

79
00:08:06,590 --> 00:08:11,590
First, make sure all the joints on the apparatus are tight and that the water lines are secured.

80
00:08:11,590 --> 00:08:17,590
Second, fill the distilling flask between 1 third and 1 half full prior to distillation.

81
00:08:17,590 --> 00:08:22,590
Third, allow the distillation to proceed at a rate of 10 drops per minute.

82
00:08:22,590 --> 00:08:32,590
Fourth, use separate receiving flasks for the beginning, middle, and end of the distillation to disallow low and high boiling impurities into the distillate.

83
00:08:32,590 --> 00:08:36,590
Fifth, never let the distillation flask run dry.

84
00:08:36,590 --> 00:08:40,590
A flask that is allowed to run dry may overheat and break.

85
00:08:40,590 --> 00:08:44,590
The second type of distillation that we will cover is the fractional distillation.

86
00:08:44,590 --> 00:08:52,590
This technique should be used if you need to separate two liquids that have a small difference in boiling point, less than 25 degrees Celsius.

87
00:08:52,590 --> 00:09:00,590
A fractional distillation is more effective at separating liquids with similar boiling points because it utilizes a fractionating column.

88
00:09:00,590 --> 00:09:09,590
A fractionating column contains column packing with a large surface area and works by promoting a lot of tiny distillations on the surface of this packing.

89
00:09:09,590 --> 00:09:16,590
Specifically, when the liquid mixture is heated to boiling, the resulting vapor is richer in the lower boiling component.

90
00:09:16,590 --> 00:09:22,590
The vapor moves out of the flask and condenses on the bottom few centimeters of the fractionating column.

91
00:09:22,590 --> 00:09:29,590
Now, the condensed droplet is richer in lower boiling component and the liquid in the distilling flask is richer in the higher boiling component.

92
00:09:29,590 --> 00:09:35,590
If that liquid droplet is reheated, it will vaporize and condense a little further up the fractionating column.

93
00:09:35,590 --> 00:09:40,590
This distillate will have an even higher percentage of the lower boiling component.

94
00:09:40,590 --> 00:09:48,590
This process is repeated until the vapor reaches the top of the fractionating column where, theoretically, it should be pure in lower boiling component.

95
00:09:48,590 --> 00:09:57,590
To assemble the apparatus for a fractional distillation, place a fractionating column between the distillation flask and the distillation head.

96
00:09:57,590 --> 00:10:03,590
Alternatively, your lab may have all-in-one distillation glassware that contains a fractionating column.

97
00:10:03,590 --> 00:10:08,590
Perform the fractional distillation in the same manner as the simple distillation.

98
00:10:08,590 --> 00:10:14,590
To ensure an effective fractional distillation, heat the distilling flask very slowly.

99
00:10:14,590 --> 00:10:23,590
Too much heat causes the distillation to occur too rapidly, disallowing the liquid vapor equilibria on the surface of the fractionating column.

100
00:10:23,590 --> 00:10:31,590
If too little heat is used, then the column may lose heat faster than it can be warmed up by the vapor, preventing the vapor from reaching the top of the column.

101
00:10:31,590 --> 00:10:40,590
If the column seems to be losing heat faster than it can be warmed up by the vapor, you may insulate the column by wrapping it with glass wool or cotton and aluminum foil.

102
00:10:40,590 --> 00:10:47,590
As a general rule, a slow, steady distillation where one drop is collected every two to three seconds is reasonable.

103
00:10:47,590 --> 00:10:54,160
Distillation is an effective technique for separating and purifying liquids.

104
00:10:54,160 --> 00:10:58,160
When used correctly, pure compounds and good yields are readily attained.
